 A striking, narrow, upright evergreen with intense silver-blue foliage and a naturally columnar form. Extremely cold hardy, wind resistant, and well adapted to Commerce City's alkaline soils and dry conditions. Provides exceptional year-round structure, privacy screening, and windbreak value. Requires good drainage in clay — see Other Care for planting specs.
]

=== Watering

Use 1 × 2 gph emitter (~0.93 gal/cycle at the current 28 min/zone runtime). Wichita Blue Juniper is drought tolerant but still in its second establishment season. In clay soil, overwatering is a greater risk than underwatering — the schedule's dry-down interval between cycles is beneficial for this plant. Never allow water to pool at or near the root crown.

=== Fertilization

Very low need. Primary annual input is the May chelated micronutrient blend. A light spring liquid N-K pass is optional, not required, and should be skipped in vigorous-growth years.

=== Pruning & Maintenance

Lightly shape in late winter or late summer only if needed to maintain columnar form. Never cut back into bare, brown interior wood — junipers will not regenerate from old wood. Light tip pruning is fine. Remove dead wood and clear accumulated dead interior needles annually for air circulation, which reduces moisture retention and bagworm habitat.

=== Wildlife Value

Wichita Blue Juniper is one of the most wildlife-valuable evergreens in your landscape. The silver-blue berries are eaten by European collared doves, American robins, common starlings, and house finches throughout winter when other food is scarce. Northern flickers investigate the bark and crevices for insects. The dense evergreen foliage provides critical winter shelter and nesting cover for house finches and other small birds — this tree offers warmth and wind protection in cold weather that few other plants match. Cottontail rabbits typically avoid junipers. Field mice may shelter at the base; maintain a clear 6-inch mulch-free zone around the trunk.

=== Pests & Disease

#link("https://www.google.com/search?q=bagworms+juniper+treatment")[Bagworms] are the primary pest — silky, cone-shaped cases appear in June; remove by hand immediately (do not wait). If infestation is heavy, spray with Bt (Bacillus thuringiensis) in June when caterpillars are small; spinosad or pyrethroid for larger infestations. #link("https://www.google.com/search?q=spider+mites+juniper+treatment")[Spider mites] can flare in hot, dry conditions — look for stippled grey-blue needles and fine webbing; treat with horticultural oil or miticide. #link("https://www.google.com/search?q=phomopsis+twig+blight+juniper+treatment")[Phomopsis twig blight] (a fungal disease) causes tip die-back in wet springs; prune infected tips with sterilized tools and improve air circulation.

=== Other Care

At planting time (or if replanting), excavate a wide, shallow hole (wider than deep), amend the entire zone with 30–40% coarse grit or perlite, and plant the root crown 1–2 inches above surrounding grade. Do not create a bowl that collects water. Maintain good air circulation around the base — remove any accumulated debris. Protect from winter road salt spray if near paved areas. Highly deer resistant.
